  "notes": "The bassist-producer inside a band, who both plays on and produces the records. The documented date is \"the clearest picture of a modern Nashville live tracking session in this file\" and it is a player count, not an adjective: \"seven players cutting together (two guitarists, drummer, bassist, scratch vocalist, grand pianist on a Yamaha C7, synthesizer player), in a drum room chosen specifically because the producer was 'convinced that the studio's acoustics would be ideal for the album's drum sound.'\" The bass is his own part and is written rather than charted: \"root motion with pop-style anticipations, 8ths under choruses, and the bass part written as part of the arrangement rather than tracked to a chart,\" which is a high anticipation figure over high root-following — the pop-rock inverse of the walking-line producers here. The spatial practice is a real arrangement fact and it is why the harmonic instruments carry more room than the kit: \"splitting the harmonic instruments into different acoustic environments — piano recorded in a marble-floored living room with a video feed, keyboards in a separate production suite, while the rhythm section stays in the dry room.\" Keys are documented: \"major with real minor-tonic singles (F minor, E♭ minor, D♭ minor all appear); vocabulary I–V–vi–IV, vi–IV–I–V, and i–VI–III–VII on the minor cuts.\" Three-part close harmony on choruses is the counter's job. ⚠ Confidence is medium (research: high-medium). ⚠ The INFERRED parameters are the velocity tiers, the perc density and the counter ratio.",
